"Lira's arm, and her ankle. Karis's throat. Brom's hands. Six bolts, at noon: three in the sleeve, one on the string, one a yard from that stone, and one gone. The box is whole, and there are eleven sheets in it and on her."

He had come to himself. He looked at the healer when he said it, since she was the one of the six who had no reason to know what she had been looking at.

"Mine's a calf, and an arm that's been struck. And what went up that hill with a light was me. I did it on purpose, to take them off the lane. I've no more to say about it tonight than that it was done."

Oryn looked at him for as long as it took her to draw one breath. She had seen a boy burst sideways out of a file with a crack like a whip, in a place where she herself could not so much as count; she had seen fire in his fist. He waited for what every person with a seal had asked him since a district hall.

"Is that calf worse than this arm?"

"No."

"Is anything you've got worse than this arm?"

"No."

"Then hold the light still and be quiet."

She shut her eyes. Her hands settled, with the fingers overlapping, and her face went as quiet as a pond.

---

He held the lantern for an hour, and for the whole of it he watched her face.

He did not watch the arm. He had seen what was to be seen of that at the head of a lane, and he found that he had no wish to learn how it looked when the linen was off. A healer on a mountain had grown a little older while he looked at her, over a shin. It was more than a little now. The sweat came out along her cropped hair in the first minutes and ran, though the frost was coming down, and by the middle of the hour the skin under her eyes had gone the colour of a bruise; her breath came slowly, at the pace that she walked.

Lira did not speak. She had been told once to lie still and let it, and she lay still. Her eyes were open, and they were on him.

He could not read what was in them. He had been able to read that face across a yard since a lane in Ardenmere, and tonight he could not. It was not anger, which he knew, and it was not fear either. She looked at him steadily, by the light of a brass lamp, as she might have looked at a card that had come up with a name on it she had not expected, while a stranger's hands took six weeks out of her arm in an hour.

He had an hour, too, in which the bills came in.

His left forearm went first. The ringing had gone out of the bone before he reached the stone, and something duller had come into its place, which spread; by the end of the first quarter he could not have told by feel where his fingers were. He set the lantern's ring over that wrist, like a bucket on a hook, and held the wrist up with the other hand.

His hands were cold, both of them, with a cold that the night did not account for. He knew whose that was. It had come down a stair with a boy, long ago, in another person's life, and it had taken something out of the air for ten strides and kept a little of him for its trouble.

Something in his right arm had begun to ask about the lantern's rim. *Edge?* It had asked that question of half the world for a season, patiently, and tonight for the first time it had been told yes; and now it asked like a child that has been given one sweet. He told it no, and it asked again.

The heaviest of them lay in him like wet sand. He had put three lanes down a ditch, and he could feel every one.

Under all of that the calf burned, slowly, in its own blood. He looked at none of it for long. He had opened a purse and counted out what was in it, and here was the reckoning, an item at a time, on his own body; and every item was smaller than the one that the healer had her hands on.

Nothing came out of the grass.

He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped; the wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the black linen in the heap.

Seven animals were somewhere in that dark which hunted the very thing that a square woman was doing with her eyes shut, and they did not come. He did not know why. He set it beside a great many other things that he did not know, and held the lamp.

When her hands opened, she did not take them away at once. She turned the arm over in them, toward the light, and looked.

It was closed. That was all that he let himself see: skin, where there had been none, and on it two short rows of small pink marks, one row on the upper side and one beneath, each mark no bigger than a barleycorn.

"Fingers," said Oryn.

Lira moved them. They all went.

"Well?" said Brom, from the north. He had not turned round. His voice was not steady.

"She keeps it."

Brom said something to the dark that was not a word. Cael found that the lantern was shaking, and that it was not his dead arm that shook it.

The healer had not finished. She kept hold of the wrist, and she bent over Lira until her face was a hand's breadth away, and she spoke as she had spoken to Brom about a cuff, slowly, so that there should be no second meaning in any piece of it.

"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."

She sat back.

"Blanket. One of the hold's. And a knife."

It was Lira's own knife that Cael gave her, since it was the one in his belt. She set it to the hem of a grey blanket and drew, and the wool dented under it and did not part. She tried it again nearer the hilt. Then she held the blade to the lantern and ran her thumb along it, squarely, as nobody would have dared to do that morning.

"Who's been cutting stone with this?"

Nobody answered her, and she did not seem to want an answer. She held out her hand to the south, and a narrow dull knife came into it out of the dark, haft first. That one cut. She took a strip off the blanket a hand wide and as long as her two arms, and passed it under Lira's elbow and round her neck, and tied it short, so that the mended arm lay across the breast with its hand under the opposite collarbone.

Lira looked down at it. She had been the quickest pair of hands on any floor that she had ever stood on.

"One arm. From here to the snow."

She said it as Brom had once said the hour of a week, to have it exact. Then her eyes went down the length of herself to the great boot, which lay on its side in the grass with the foot still in it, and came up again; and she shut her mouth.

The healer had followed the look.

"The ankle's a tape and a season. I've told you that before."

"I didn't ask."

"No. You were about to not ask, and I'd sooner say it than watch you." Oryn's voice had gone to a thread. "I'll tape it in the morning. I've nothing left to see it with."

She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.

---

There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.

Seln brought them down, since he was the only one with two hands and nothing wrong with either, and he laid two on Lira and one on the healer where she had fallen. He cut the bread as well, a round loaf from the hold's oven, into six, with the same knife that had cut the sling; and he left the sixth piece on a pannier lid by Oryn's head, where she would find it before she found anything else.

Nobody made a fire. Nobody proposed one.

Karis had her notebook open on her knee, and she was writing with the pencil held in her fist. Her knuckles were skinned across both hands, where teeth had gone over them; she had looked at them once and left them. When she had three lines she turned the leaf to the lantern and to him.

*Throat: pressed, not cut. Air: enough. Voice: none whatever. Hands: skin.*

*Wall: nine points, eight joins, sixty yards. Count of six. Yours. Not empty: a tenth part left, perhaps. I have done as I was told.*

*Sheets: eleven. Dry. Not looked at.*

He read them, and nodded. She took the leaf back and held the pencil over it, and he watched the point come down to the paper and lift, and come down. He knew what was in that small head. She had been ten strides in front of him with a hound's jaw across her fists, and she had seen a thing arrive that had not come; and after it a crack, and a light, and something that rang. She had nine questions at the least, and she had been waiting all her life for a source like this one.

She wrote one line more, and turned it to him.

*I am asking nothing tonight. Entered.*

Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything: the healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning, Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.

She came across to him on her knees, with what remained of the blanket strip, and took his boot into her lap.

She bound the calf as she would have ruled a table. He supposed that she had watched a healer do it under a rock, and had kept it, as she kept everything; she was not gentle, and she was very exact, and when she tied it off the knot lay flat. She patted the leg once, absently, as she had patted a box on Brom's back. Then she went and lay down on her side against that box with her back to all of them, in Seln's coat, as she had lain on a mountain.

"Seln," said Cael. "Say yours."

Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.

"Nothing."

"Not a scratch?"

"I have looked. I find that I mind it rather more than I had expected to." He inclined his head, as to a report that had been received, and went back to the south.

Brom had come in. He did not wait to be asked. He knelt down heavily beside the sleeping healer, box and all, and laid the bow in the grass with the bolt still on it, and held out both his hands into the light, palms up.

"Hand."

Across the pads of four fingers on each there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled, and in two of them it had gone deep. They had bled down into his cuffs and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in them.

Oryn did not open her eyes. Her lips moved, in the trodden grass.

"Morning. Wash it. I've nothing tonight."

"I wasn't asking for tonight," said Brom. "I was telling you there was one." He looked down at her with an expression that Cael had last seen on him at the edge of a moss. "Go to sleep. You're asleep."

He washed them himself, from the skin, with his teeth set, and wound a strip of the sling's leavings round each; and he took up the bow again in those two bundles and went back to the north.

That was the camp. Nobody had said a word to Cael about a hill. They had eaten bread beside him, and passed him the water, and Seln had put the last blanket round his shoulders without a remark, as he would have put it round anybody. Four of them knew what he was and had not known until tonight that it reached in there. One of them knew nothing, and had asked him whether his calf was worse than an arm.

He had expected questions, and he had been given a blanket, and a sixth of a loaf, and a knot that lay flat. He did not know yet which he would have found the easier to bear.

---

He wrote while there was oil.

Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, and he took it that she meant hours. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.

He did not go back to finish it. He began a new one beneath.

*Ninety-sixth day, night. West of the stone, on the old road.*

*Nine hounds, inside. Two dead, both Brom's, with a healer's bow that he had never shot: one on the floor, at thirteen feet, with my hands on its head; one over the stone, at six. Seven out, north and south along the line's outside. Not seen after. No instruments were there. No price.*

*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: eight fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*

*Mine: left calf open on the old seam. Left forearm struck; dead to the elbow. Ribs taped, three days to run; they held.*

Then he ruled a line, and under it he put down what he had spent, in the order of its spending, with what each thing had cost and on whom the cost had fallen. He had never had so many to write at once, and he gave each of them its own line, as Pike gave a job.

*The read: far lip to the rut. One head turned for it. I had my eyes in the ground when Lira went up on the rim.*

*Shadow: once, ten strides, in the lane. Four saw it who have always known. The fifth had a man and a box between. I did that sum before, not after. It holds. Cold hands.*

*Wind: a burst, north off the lane. The lock.*

*Pressure: once, into the ground. The tape.*

*Ember: one point, on a knife, lane to stone. Nothing seen beyond ten feet of it. A blister where the hilt was.*

*Blade: once. An edge on that knife. The knife won't cut wool.*

*Anchor: looking, nothing fixed. It showed me nine points before they lit.*

*Storm: three lanes, in the rut. The read goes to the air for those. That's the calf.*

*Wind: the second burst. The lock had a hound in it.*

*Compression: in that lock, late. That's the arm.*

He went down the ten lines with the pencil's end and counted uses, taking the three lanes singly, and had twelve.

*Twelve. With the ford, thirteen.*

*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*

It stood where the word *one* had stood on forty-three nights. He had shut the book at a far lip because he did not know what would be true by dark; and here was a true number, and a large one, and there was not a unit in it that had not been paid for on somebody's body, his own or another's.

It was not the whole. He looked at that squarely, with the pencil lifted. There were things that he had done at noon, by himself, a cord north of this stone and on a floor, which nothing had seen and which he had written of only to say that he had told nobody. They were not in the twelve. He would not put them into a count that he could not yet explain, and he would not pretend to the page that they had not happened.

*Not in that number: noon. It's on the leaf before this. It gets a line of its own when I can say what it was for.*

There was one thing more, and it was the only part of the day that he could write as plainly as a width.

*Everyone else's went to sleep at the stone. Mine has been asleep since I was fourteen. Out here that makes me the only one who's awake.*

He read it over. It was the kind of sentence that Karis struck out of other people's notes, since it had a likeness in it. He let it stand, since he had nothing truer.

---

"It worked in there."

He had thought her asleep. He had put the lantern out to save Karis's three fingers, and the stars had come down close over the grass; she lay on her back under two blankets, with her mended arm tied across her, and her face was a pale shape turned up to them.

He did not answer. It had not been a question.

He heard her breathe. She was doing what she had done in a loft at Thornwater, before a ford, when he gave her a plan that she disliked: going through it, a piece at a time, to find the place where she could make it better.

"Yours."

It was one word, and she had left him the whole width of it. She did not say which of them, or how many. She did not say that she had been looking at grass on her left hand, in a lane, with nobody in it.

"Yes," Cael said.

The mule shifted. Somewhere to the north Brom cleared his throat, and was quiet.

He waited for the next thing, and found that he was holding his breath as he had held it under a healer's hand. He had a great many sentences ready for her, and not one of them was an answer. He did not know why. He had not known at noon, and he had kept that from her at noon with a pencil going; and she, of all the people alive, had the right to ask him how long.

"Later."

She said it up into the dark. There was no hardness in it. It was a thing put by on a shelf, with its face outward, where neither of them could pretend that it was not there.

"Later."

She turned her head a little, then, toward him, and he thought that she was going to say something else. She did not. She shut her eyes; and presently her breathing lengthened, and he knew by it that the arm had stopped hurting her enough to let her go.

He sat on beside her with a dead arm across his knee.

The seven did not come, and the stone lay where it had lain, and behind it, a long way off up the slope, there was a faint paleness that he would not have seen if he had not known where to look. He watched the stars go over, and tried to make the sentence that she had given him leave to wait for.

He could not make it. He had the fact, which was that nine things had answered him in a place where a healer could not count. He had nothing to put after the fact. And he knew what became of a thing that was written down before anybody understood it, since it had been done to him: a clerk had found no tier for a boy, and had put a word in capitals where the tier should go, and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was. A file stopped the asking. He would not do that to this, and he would not hide it either.

It was the better part of two hours before he opened the book again; and he wrote by starlight, large, one sentence to a line, as Karis had written at this same stone on the night before.

*I'm not keeping it secret. I'm keeping it unfiled. There's a difference. Lira said later. Later is when I have a sentence. The answer might be: because whatever I am isn't a Path. I've known that since I was fourteen. Out there it was a flag. In there it's the only thing that works. I don't have a word for what that makes me, and I'm not going to invent one at a camp with Lira's arm two hours old.*

He shut the book on it, and lay down, and did not sleep for some time.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md

# Chapter 34 — Maps with Holes


exec
/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '1001,1250p' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-cold-prompt.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
It was that same night, the ninety-sixth going into the ninety-seventh, and Seln had the south.

He stood forty yards from the lamp, in grass to his hip, where its light did not reach him and his own eyes were not spoiled by it. He had the line on his left hand. He could not see it. He knew where it was by a strip of burnt stems that began ten yards from his boot, and by a thing behind his breastbone that had gone out at a stone on the way in, and had been there again at the same stone on the way out, exactly where he had left it.

He had called a word up a hill tonight, at the top of his voice, and he could not remember when he had last raised it.

The camp was quiet behind him. He listened to it as he listened to any room that he had his back to, and sorted what he heard: a pencil, and then no pencil; a big man washing his hands without a sound; and the healer's breathing, which was slow and went all the way down.

"Seln. Say yours."

The boy had asked it an hour ago, across the lamp.

He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.

Tonight he had been asked, by name, for the first time. And the whole of what he had to hand in was one word.

It was an accurate word. He had submitted a great many accurate reports, and few of them had sat so ill.

He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.

It had been right. A bow at the lip had been worth more than a second knife, and he would have gone again. He noted only that it was the same price each time, and that somebody else always paid it. He had no column for that, and he found, a little to his surprise, that he would have liked one.

There was the other thing, and he came to it when he had finished with himself, as he came to the difficult page of any file.

He had been looking straight back up the lane. That was his place; the man on point looks behind him as often as before. He had seen the small one go down, and a grey animal step out between her and his knife, and beyond those the two women, and the box. Behind the box there had been a boy with a blade in his hand.

Then there had been the lane, and the grass, and the box.

Seln had not seen him go. Nobody sees that. It is the whole of the craft that there is no instant at which a person is seen to leave; the eye passes over the place and finds it not worth resting on, and goes to something with more in it. He had learned it on a back stair when he was younger than any of them. It had taken him a year to do on purpose, and three more to stop doing by accident whenever a door slammed.

He had known it this time from the outside, by two signs. A big man's eye had slid over a piece of air and come back to it. And along the south edge of the lane the grass had leaned, stem by stem, with nothing leaning on it.

It had gone on for ten strides. Then there had been a boy with both hands on a hound.

Seln had done the sum himself, in the half-breath before. It was the first thing that a person of his trade did on entering any room, and he had done it without choosing to: who is here, and what can each of them see, and where will each of them be tomorrow. There were four who had known for years and had been tried. There was a healer, who was owed everything and knew nothing, and who walked seven roads. She had her back turned and a hundredweight of copied stone between.

He would have said *go*. He had not needed to. The boy had stood there for as long as a jaw takes to shut, doing the same sum, and had got the same answer; and he had lifted it, and carried it exactly as far as it was wanted, and put it down with his hands already full.

It was correct. Seln looked for a better word and did not find one.

He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.

He did not propose to say so to anyone.

The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.

---

The stars went over, and the frost came down, and toward the end of the watch he let himself think about the iron.

He had been keeping off it since noon. It had stood at the back of everything that he did that day, as a figure stands at the back of a room: a square bar, driven at the far end of a measure, with a stamp on its neck that he had seen on every door he had walked through for fifteen years.

Thirty-one.

He had told the boy that he did not know what the number counted, and that he would not guess. Both of those were true. There was a third thing which he had not said, because it was not yet a thing: something in him had answered to that iron, and it was not the Shadow.

He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.

The big man came down out of the north when the east had begun to grey, walking as a person walks who has stood since midnight, with a bow in two bundles of rag.

"Yours is over," said Brom. "Nothing?"

"Nothing."

"Same. I'd sooner there'd been something. I'd know where it was."

"That is very well put," said Seln, and meant it.

He did not go to his blanket. He went along the line to the north, with the burnt strip on his right hand and then the unburnt grass, counting Brom's turfs as he came to them. They lay grass downward, each a cord from the last, and the frost had made them grey. At the third he stopped.

It stood where it had stood. He crouched over it without touching it, as he had crouched at noon, and waited for the light.

It came slowly. First there was a bar, and then the head of it, spread by a mallet. Then there was the stamp.

He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.

A case with a strap.

The drawer came open.

It was a tin, as long as his forearm, with a cap at one end and a lead tag crimped through the hasp. He had it under his arm. He was young, in the memory, with a satchel and one key, and he was carrying the tin from one room to another; that was the whole of the errand. A junior was permitted to break the tag in the second room, before a senior, and to draw out what was in the tin and count it against a docket. He was to count. He was not to read.

There had been eight sheets. They were fair copies, on paper backed with linen, and they were of the east.

He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.

And on some of the sheets, out in the white, there had been a round.

It was a small exact circle, ruled with a compass. He remembered the prick of the point at its middle, where the paper had been dented. There was nothing inside it at all; a dashed road came up to one and went about it and on. At the foot of each sheet there had been a little table of signs, a ford, a hold, a hill, and no sign among them for a round with nothing in it. On one sheet there were two.

He had counted eight, and said *eight*, and the senior had initialled the docket; and a young man with a satchel had gone down a stair thinking about his dinner.

He had taken those rounds for what a junior takes everything for, which is a thing that he has not been told. He had supposed a post, or a store, with a guard on it. It had never once entered his head that a place might be left off a map because of what the ground itself did there.

Seln stayed on his heels by the iron a long while, and went over what he had, strictly, as he would have gone over it for somebody else's signature. He had eight sheets, seen once, by a boy who was counting. He had rounds, ruled, with a road going about one of them. He had a stake under his eyes, of the pattern that is signed for, driven on the edge of a circle that a researcher had paced with her feet.

He did not have the leaf. There would be one. A sheet with a hole in it has a page somewhere that says what the hole is for; that was how every office that he had ever served drew breath. He had never seen it. He did not know what room it lay in, or under what heading, or whose hand had ruled those rounds. He knew the weight of a tin and the number of its sheets, and he knew that he would be asked for more than that before the sun was high, by a small person with a pencil and no voice.

He could not tell, from memory, where on those sheets a round had fallen. He had the look of one: a water crossed, and dashes running on east of it, and then the white place. To say more than that he needed a ford drawn by somebody who had put a wheel through it.

He stood up, and his knees cracked in the cold.

The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.

He went down on one knee in front of her, so that she should not have to lift her chin.

"I should like the carter's map," said Seln. "And the charcoal, if it came east in that box."

---

The healer did her two at noon.

She had woken at first light and eaten the sixth of a loaf that lay by her head, and slept again, on purpose, for two hours by the sun. Then she had got up and washed to the elbow from the skin, and taped Lira's foot without a word, with an anchor turn and two strips up the outside and the long one crossed in front. After that she had sat with her back against a pannier and her eyes on the top of the sky.

"I'll do them when it's straight up," she said. "You've all told me that's the hour those things have never been seen about. If I'm to shout twice more I'll do it then."

Cael watched it from the grass, with his leg out.

She took Karis first. Two fingers went to the inside of a thin wrist, and the other hand lay along the front of the throat, lightly, where there was a bruise the shape of two fists. Her lips went to ten.

"Days. Hoarse before it's anything."

Karis's hand moved, for a pencil that she had put down.

"No. Don't write it. I can see what you want to know." The healer shifted her palm a little. "It's pressed, and it's swollen, and I can take the swelling down and start the rest. I can't give you the voice today. It comes back by itself, rough first, and you'll leave it alone until it does. You don't whisper. A whisper's harder on it than talking."

She shut her eyes. It took her a quarter of an hour, and when her hands came away the bruise had gone from purple to yellow, as a week does it, and Karis swallowed, and swallowed again, with a look of astonishment.

"That's one."

Brom was beside her already, on his knees, with the rags unwound. The cuts had dried black across eight finger-pads, and the two deep ones stood a little open.

[[TIER-B: Oryn mending Brom's string-cut fingers at noon on the 97th, "String… That's two." — coordinator to supply protected text]]

It was quicker than the throat. When she let go of him there were eight thin pale lines where the cuts had been, and he closed both fists, slowly, and opened them.

"You draw nothing with those till the hundredth day. Not a bow, not a rope, not a lace if you can find somebody else to pull it. They're skin. They're not hide yet."

"The hundredth," said Brom. "That's three days. I'll know when."

"I know you will. I've met you."

She sat back, and Cael saw what the pair of them had cost her. She had been grey when she woke, and she was grey again. A Tide on a road had two in her, and she had spent the whole of yesterday's on one arm.

"Leg," she said.

He had not expected it. He had thought that she would take her two hours first; but she was looking at the calf, where a strip of grey blanket had gone dark in the night, and she held out her hand for it as for a tool.

"Who tied this?"

Karis lifted one skinned hand.

"It's a good knot. It's the wrong cloth." She had it off, and she spent very little time over what was under it. She washed it from the skin, and it ran, and she put both her hands round the leg above the place and below. Her lips began to move.

He counted with her, as he had counted on a mountain, and on a scrubbed table, twice. *One. Two.* He knew now what he had not known on any of those days. He had stood on a hill in the dark with every one of the nine going out of him at once, and she had been standing on a lane below him with her eyes open.

*Five. Six.*

At six her lips stopped.

Her hands stayed where they were. She shifted her thumbs, and pressed, and he saw the old crease come between her brows. She had the look of a person trying a door that she knows to be locked, for the sake of having tried it.

"I can't mend what I can't read. That's the rule."

"You'd a night's sleep," said Lira. She had come up on her good elbow. "And you've seen more of him than you had. Try it again."

"I've seen him run up a hill. That's not the same as reading him." Oryn took her hands away and wiped them on her breeches. "And I don't say a finding four times to a patient and change it on the fifth."

Her eyes came up to his, level and tired and a little hungry, as they had come up once over three broken ribs.

"It's closing. I can see it closing. It opened last night and it's half shut, with nothing doing it, the same as those ribs." She reached for clean linen. "You get what a carter gets. Again."

She bound it in her own cloth, tight and neat, and she put no question to him of any kind.

---

Seln came for them in the afternoon.

He had been at the stake since first light, by himself, with a calfskin and a stick of charcoal and a sheet of Karis's clean paper. Cael had watched him from the camp: a narrow dark figure three cords up the line, sitting on its heels, that drew a little and then sat for a long while with its hands hanging.

"I have something to show," he said. "I should like it shown where the iron is. It is three cords, and she may take my arm."

They went at Lira's pace. She walked it flat, in the great boot, with her right hand on Seln's sleeve and her left tied across her breast; and Cael came behind on a leg that gave him eleven strides and then shut like a fist, as it had done once before, on a green rock in a river. He stood until it let go. It was a count that he knew.

The healer had come too. She had not been asked. She looked at the square brown bar in the grass, and at the stamp on its neck, for about as long as she had looked at five people under a rock; and then she let herself down beside it, by stages, and sat with her knees up and the iron against the small of her back.

"That's cold," she said. She did not move.

[[TIER-B: Oryn sitting against the stake on the 97th, summing her mendings, "Three in two days… That's a route." — coordinator to supply protected text]]

Cael had sat down across from her, with his leg out. He waited to learn whether she had more.

"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."

He had been counting, since a far lip, what the day had cost. He had Lira's arm in it, and a throat, and eight fingers; he had his own leg, and a number in a book. He had not had this. A woman had added a stop to the only thing she owned, for two reasons of her own, and the stop had taken three mendings in two days from seven gates that had not been asked.

"I'd not have had it," he said. "I have it now."

"Good. Don't do anything with it. Just have it."

She put her head back against nothing, and shut her eyes.

---

Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.

"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."

He took up the sheet of paper. It was thin stuff, the kind that Karis had rubbed stones with, and when he laid it over the hide the carter's brown ink showed through it faintly.

"I have traced three things from his map, and no more. Here is the first water; those are his three strokes. Here is his square with a dot, which is the hold we slept at. And here is his day's measure, a tick to a day, so that what I draw shall be on his scale and not on mine."

He lifted the sheet away and laid it on his knee.

"The rest I have put in from memory, with the paper off the skin, so that I could not be led. On the sheets that I counted, a road went out from the Line in dashes. It crossed a water. It ran on east. And at a certain distance past the crossing, the dashes went round a white place, ruled with a compass, that had nothing in it."

He had drawn it in charcoal. Cael saw a row of short strokes going across the thin paper from left to right, and two days beyond a small square they bent, and went about a neat black ring, and came back.

Seln laid the paper down on the calfskin again, and fitted the three wavy strokes to the three beneath.

Nobody spoke.

There was a pencilled ring on the hide, where Karis had drawn it on the ninety-second evening, round a patch of bare skin that a carter had left when he bent his road. The charcoal ring lay on it. It was a little the larger. They had one middle.

"They surveyed it," Seln said.

His voice was as mild as it had ever been, and Cael had known him two years and had never heard it so cold.

"Five years ago by the stake, maybe six. They paced the circles and stamped the stakes and drew the maps with holes where the circles are. That's not ignorance. Ignorance doesn't stamp iron."

"Circles," said Lira. "You said circles. More than this."

"There was a sheet with two. I cannot tell you where the second lay, or the third, if there was a third. I counted corners." His eyes went to the two small rings on the hide beside which Karis had written *guess*, and came away. "I shall not promote a guess of hers on a memory of mine."

Brom was looking at the iron, behind the healer's back.

"Who's *they*? You keep saying it. The registry's a room full of clerks. A clerk didn't walk two days past a fence and drive that in."

Seln was silent for a breath. There was a word that not one of the five had said aloud since they crossed the Line, and Cael saw him decide to say it.

"The Compact knows what quiet ground does."

It lay on the afternoon like the iron itself. Cael looked at the stamp, a thumb's breadth below the beaten head.

"And it's filed somewhere we can't reach."

Karis had the notebook on her knee. She wrote one word, large, and turned it to him.

*Where*


