+# Movement 005 review — simulated cold read
+
+## Status and coverage
+
+This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement and not a self-review. I read only the seven supplied manuscript files, chapters 31–37, in their declared order. No editorial canon was supplied, so I can identify apparent internal consistency or inconsistency but cannot verify canon violations, plan adherence, or reserved-revelation compliance.
+
+The supplied movement reaches a clear dramatic endpoint, but the manuscript text is not cleanly complete: four visible `TIER-B` coordinator placeholders remain in chapters 31, 34 (two), and 37. Those gaps lower confidence where they interrupt Oryn's healing and the long reading.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught immediately with “Seln stood up, and the floor went white” (chapter 31, line 3). The physical problem is concrete before the full metaphysics are clear: six injured or limited people, a white floor that suppresses their Paths, nine hounds, and 233 yards to safety. The hounds' indifference to ordinary resistance is more disturbing than simple aggression. The movement becomes fully compelling when Cael realizes that the pack has never needed to learn because nothing inside the ring has ever been able to “shout,” then chooses to become the loudest thing there (chapter 32, lines 31 and 151–181).
+
+The action in chapters 31–32 is the strongest stretch. Terrain, body mechanics, available tools, injuries, distances, ammunition, and power costs all matter. The escape develops by observation and adaptation: the rolled lip spoils a hound's spring, lifting the hindquarters breaks a bite, Cael's declaration draws the pack, the rut channels Storm, and Karis's fire converts the boundary into a barrier. The result feels earned rather than granted.
+
+My attention slipped most after the escape, during the repeated accounting in late chapter 33 through early chapter 35. The recovery is emotionally necessary, and several of its quiet beats are excellent, but injuries, power expenditures, day counts, and supplies are sometimes narrated, entered in a notebook, and then summarized again. The four unresolved placeholders also cause abrupt, unmistakable breaks, especially at the bite treatment in chapter 31 and during the central reading in chapter 37.
+
+Oryn matters most to me by the end of the movement. She begins as the practical healer whose hands fail inside the ring, spends herself restoring Lira, tests the boundary privately for her own understanding, and becomes both the source and the wronged party in Cael's tenth acquisition. Cael remains the narrative center, while Lira is the relationship that gives his secrecy emotional weight. Brom and Karis repeatedly keep the group from becoming grimly functional: his refusal to abandon the copied sheets, her precise written jokes, and their separate statements that Cael's intended act is wrong make them feel like people rather than support pieces.
+
+I expect the next event to be Oryn hearing the full truth and ruling on what Cael did, including whether the acquisition diminished or altered her. Behind that immediate reckoning, I expect the westbound report that six people returned from the blank circle to activate Compact attention. I would continue. The ending—Oryn keeping her hands in place and demanding minute twenty-one—lands as both invitation and judgment without settling either one too early.
+
+## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
+
+This is an editorial simulation of that reading perspective, not evidence from a thirteen-year-old reader.
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8/10 | Chapter 31, lines 3–37, from “the floor went white” through the arrival and count of nine hounds | The visual change, trapped group, and immediate count create danger quickly. Some terms—Path, read, floor, cord—must be inferred from prior-book context, so the movement is gripping before it is fully legible. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 32, lines 293–317, the fire wall and “Somebody was striking a flint”; chapter 37, lines 226–244, “Did I do that?” through minute twenty-one | Each local crisis pays off while opening a sharper next question: first survival and exposure, then truth and consent. The chapter 37 ending strongly demands the next scene. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 34, lines 210–259, the traced blank circle and “The Compact knows what quiet ground does” | A progression system failing inside mapped holes, while the protagonist's anomalous fragments still work, feels specific. The hidden-survey revelation turns landscape into institutional threat. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Chapter 31, lines 175–286, Lira's run, bite, restraint, and Brom's shot; chapter 37, lines 67–82, the long-reading placeholder and return to the scene | The physical action is unusually trackable, but exact distances, counts, specialized terms, and long causal sentences demand attention. The visible placeholders break comprehension rather than merely polish. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 31, lines 175–215, Lira choosing to draw the hounds; chapter 33, lines 89–149, Oryn saving the arm and Karis declining to question Cael | Sacrifice is followed by care, restrictions, and quiet reciprocity. People protect one another in distinct ways, which makes the group easy to care about. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 35, lines 225–264, Seln following Brom's bread instructions and Karis entering the result as a finding | The joke grows from established habits—Brom's cooking, Seln's literal precision, Karis's recordkeeping—and releases pressure without dismissing the injuries. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9/10 | Chapter 31, lines 124–286, the movement around the rim; chapter 32, lines 133–305, Cael's diversion, rut run, and fire barrier | Objectives, positions, limitations, adaptations, and costs remain visible through a long sequence. The repeated counting may feel dense, but it usually increases rather than blurs tension. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Chapter 32, lines 151–213, Cael releasing multiple fragments and paying their costs; chapter 37, lines 147–192, the directed tenth acquisition | Abilities learned earlier combine to solve a new problem, and the next gain changes the rules of acquisition. The reward is complicated by a clear moral cost rather than treated as free advancement. | High |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 33, lines 95–115, Lira's restrictions; chapter 35, lines 175–215, Lira stopping her left hand when Karis falls | Damage and instructions persist across chapters and alter ordinary movement. Resources, secrecy, fatigue, and relationships also carry forward rather than resetting. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 7/10 | Chapter 32, lines 181–213, from “He had a light in his fist” through the three Storm lanes; chapter 37, line 67 | Strong repeated structures and short landing sentences read well, but some long sentences stack spatial, mechanical, and emotional clauses. The literal placeholder in the reading scene is unperformable prose. | High |
+
+## Adult genre-reader lens
+
+This is an editorial simulation of that reading perspective, not demographic research.
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 9/10 | Chapter 31, lines 3–37 | The serene white floor, exact escape geometry, and unhurried pack establish dread with control. Prior context is assumed, but the movement's immediate problem is self-supporting. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 34, lines 210–282, the map's hole and the false recovery notice; chapter 37, lines 226–244 | The movement converts a survival set piece into institutional mystery, then into an intimate ethical reckoning. Both forward pressures remain active at the close. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 32, lines 151–181, power becoming detectable “noise” in quiet ground; chapter 34, lines 243–282, official knowledge encoded as blankness | The interaction among geography, magic, predators, bureaucracy, and recordkeeping is distinctive. The mystery is advanced by method rather than exposition alone. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 8/10 | Chapter 32, lines 133–305; chapter 33, lines 156–236, Cael's written accounting | The set piece is precise and causal. Flow slows when the same state is re-itemized in dialogue, narration, and journal form, and the placeholders create hard gaps. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 33, lines 53–149, Oryn's treatment and the crew's quiet care; chapter 35, lines 259–264, Cael recognizing their silence as trust | Attachment comes from choices under cost: carrying paper, spending healing, binding a leg, refusing premature questions, and telling an uncomfortable truth aloud. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 9/10 | Chapter 35, lines 225–264, the bread scene | The humor is affectionate, character-specific, and perfectly placed after sustained danger. It also proves that the group's ordinary social life survives Cael's revelation. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 10/10 | Chapter 31, lines 124–286, and chapter 32, lines 31–305 | The fight/escape has evolving tactical problems, understandable body mechanics, opponent behavior, terrain use, resource limits, and lasting costs. Its length is earned; it should not be shortened merely for pace. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Chapter 32, lines 151–213; chapter 37, lines 147–192 | Existing fragments combine in a high-cost deployment, then the acquisition model expands from combat to any sustained real engagement. The tenth fragment is emotionally consequential and functionally limited. | High |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 33, lines 101–149; chapter 35, lines 57–91 and 175–215; chapter 36, lines 115–223 | Physical state, secrecy, prior promises, and models all shape later decisions. Chapter 36's consent debate grows directly out of Oryn's care and the group's experience in the ring. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8/10 | Chapter 32, lines 151–181; chapter 37, lines 87–115, ending “He had never before been a patient” | Cadence, parallel construction, and recurring count language are strong. A few clause-heavy sentences need deliberate voicing, and placeholders prevent a clean performance. | High |
+
+## Apparent continuity and boundary check
+
+With no canon evidence supplied, none of the following is a verified canon finding.
+
+- **Physical state:** Apparent continuity is strong. Lira's bitten left arm is deeply mended, immobilized, and barred from use until first snow; her right ankle's third failure is taped and forces a flat-footed gait. Chapter 35 tests both restrictions in ordinary movement. Karis's compressed throat progresses from no voice to a rough isolated word. Brom's eight string cuts are mended but restricted until day 100. Cael's opened calf, struck forearm, taped ribs, cold hands, and delayed fragment costs all persist and then improve on stated timelines.
+- **Resources and count:** The nine hounds reduce to two dead and seven dispersed. The six bolts remain accounted for: two are fired, one is abandoned on the white floor, the other is recovered, and five are subsequently present. The eleven copied sheets remain dry and boxed. Food, blankets, mule, bow, and medical supplies carry forward.
+- **Power limits and costs:** Quiet ground suppresses ordinary Paths but not Cael's fragments. Wind's landing lock, Storm's delayed response, the exchange between ground-read and air control, Shadow's cold cost, Blade's damage to Lira's knife, and Oryn's limited mendings all affect decisions afterward. No supplied passage plainly violates a previously stated limit.
+- **Knowledge boundaries:** Lira, Brom, Karis, and Seln know Cael's underlying secret; Oryn sees overt manifestations but not their origin. Shadow is used only after Cael checks that Oryn's view is blocked. The crew preserves Lira's claim on the later explanation. Seln refuses to turn partial memory into a false map, and Karis labels claims at their evidentiary strength.
+- **Reserved disclosures:** Not verifiable without canon or an editorial packet. Within the manuscript, the large revelations are that the Compact surveyed the quiet circles, omitted them from ordinary maps, may be using recovery notices to test outcomes, and that Cael can acquire a non-hostile clinical fragment. I cannot determine whether those revelations were reserved for later.
+- **Textual incompleteness:** Chapter 31 line 293, chapter 34 lines 135 and 191, and chapter 37 line 67 contain coordinator placeholders. These are not canon contradictions, but they remove evidence at four consequential beats and prevent a fully confident cold-read judgment of Oryn's arc.
+
+## Overall judgment
+
+This is a compelling movement with a rare combination of tactical clarity, humane aftermath, and morally consequential progression. Chapters 31–32 deliver a substantial action sequence whose solutions emerge from established limitations and shared competence. Chapters 33–35 make the cost persist in bodies and relationships. Chapters 36–37 then turn the progression reward into the movement's hardest problem: Cael knowingly chooses acquisition without informed consent, after giving his friends every chance to name the act accurately.
+
+The manuscript does not excuse him outright—four characters call the choice wrong, Cael agrees, and the narration names the parallel with the registry—but one piece of his final reasoning is unstable. In chapter 37, lines 147–176, refusing the acquisition is framed as potentially keeping “the whole of the wanting” while sending back the bill, as though restraint could become a dishonest evasion. That is psychologically believable self-argument, but until Oryn is given equal interpretive authority it can also sound like the narrative converting prior confession into permission. The closing exchange is strong because it postpones the ruling to the person most entitled to make it. The next scene needs to honor that promise.
+
